critical times in sri lanka  more than 60 000 health workers are on  strike across the nation disrupting a  range of services including medical  clinics consultations and tests  nurses medical technicians and public  health inspectors are among those taking  part  we can't get medicine or treatment  because of this strike we traveled a  long way without even food because we  had to get some tests done  the indefinite work stoppage by 18  health trade unions is pushing for a  number of demands  our patients and our peoples are  suffering  regarding this but  what can we do when the health  ministries and the  public administration and psc and the  salary commission and minister of health  and secretary of health they don't  answer anything right this is uh this is  the situation of sri lankan health  system kumudesh says the strike is about  winning due recognition for workers  across the health sector who have been  neglected  he says salary discrepancies  administrative issues and the  implementation of systems dealing with  the public are among their concerns  many sri lankans rely on the free health  service because they cannot afford  private health care  some of them travel long distances to  secure specialist treatment and attend  regular clinics  rural hospitals like this are usually  teeming with people but have gone quiet  in the last three days  health minister khali rambowell met  union leaders on tuesday but they failed  to resolve the issue there are a few  genuine  cases  of  their demands some genuine cases and  i would say  they  i  accept the right to ask for them all  right but certain things i think it is  completely tainted and painted and  colored with political  aspirations  and  that  i am not prepared to tolerate  strikers have demonstrated outside the  health ministry some even forcing their  way in to urge their demands be met  while the government and health trade  unions try to negotiate a deal  thousands of sri lankans are badly  affected by disruptions to the free  public health service  many want to see the two sides working  out an agreement but observers say that  seems unlikely  fernandez al-jazeera colombo
 

